1. Read a simple microphone (MAX9814 or electret) and produce a usable sound-energy or
threshold event.
2. Drive an MP3 Trigger / voice playback module from sensor or state events to produce character
sounds.
3. Understand when to stay with a Nano + trigger approach versus moving to ESP32 + MAX98357
for higher-quality output.
4. Wire sound reactions into the existing fused-state / ROS 2 pipeline without rewriting earlier
sensor nodes.
5. Design simple, reliable “sensor event → character sound” behaviors that make the droid feel
responsive.
9. Next Steps After This Introduction
This document is the conceptual and architectural introduction. Subsequent materials in the set will
cover:
• Wiring and pin recommendations for the Nano + MP3 Trigger starting path
• Basic microphone threshold / energy detection sketches
• Triggering sound banks from existing shock, tilt, and motion events
• Optional later path: ESP32 + MAX98357 I2S setup
• Suggested sound design experiments and content-friendly demonstrations
Sound turns a capable sensor platform into a character that people want to watch and interact with.
Keeping the audio layer modular and sequenced after solid sensing work preserves the teachable,
reproducible structure that supports both the droid itself and the longer-term sustainable teaching
model.
